\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}

We fine-tune Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct on 8K multi-turn conversations from the UltraChat training split, a dataset of naturalistic dialogues across diverse topics. LoRA adapters (rank 16, $\alpha$=32) are applied to query and value projection layers (\texttt{q\_proj}, \texttt{v\_proj}). The combined loss $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_\text{NTP} + \lambda_\text{GAR}\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} + \lambda_\text{div}\mathcal{L}_\text{div}$ uses $\lambda_\text{GAR} = \lambda_\text{div} = 1.0$ with MSE target $\tau = 0.5$ (\S\ref{sec:loss}). We train for 3 epochs with AdamW (lr $= 2 \times 10^{-4}$, batch size 1, gradient accumulation 8, seed 42); each variant requires approximately 6 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (102\,GB). Full hyperparameters are provided in Appendix~A.

To isolate the contributions of gating and gradient conflict, we evaluate five configurations. \textbf{ThreadGate} is the full system: LoRA adapters on \texttt{q\_proj} and \texttt{v\_proj}, 28 independent content-and-turn-conditioned gate networks (Eq.~\ref{eq:gate}), and the complete three-term loss. \textbf{\;+\,PCGrad} adds PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad} to ThreadGate, projecting conflicting NTP and GAR gradients onto each other's normal planes; the architecture and loss are identical to ThreadGate. \textbf{Uniform Gate} retains 28 per-layer gate networks but replaces the turn embedding with a learned constant vector, removing turn conditioning while retaining content-dependent gating. \textbf{LoRA-only} fixes all gates at 1.0, trained with $\mathcal{L}_\text{NTP} + \mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ ($\lambda_\text{GAR} = 1.0$) but without $\mathcal{L}_\text{div}$. \textbf{Base} is the pretrained model without fine-tuning. ThreadGate vs.\ +PCGrad isolates gradient direction conflict; ThreadGate vs.\ Uniform Gate isolates turn conditioning; ThreadGate vs.\ LoRA-only isolates gating; LoRA-only vs.\ Base isolates fine-tuning. All fine-tuned variants share training data, LoRA configuration, optimizer settings, and random seed. Each configuration is evaluated under two teacher-forced protocols. \emph{IID evaluation} computes metrics on UltraChat text from the same distribution as training. \emph{Held-out evaluation} uses 50 non-overlapping conversations to test within-distribution generalization. We report three metrics: GAR-ours (attention to goal tokens at the last token of each assistant turn, as defined in \S\ref{sec:background}), GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention} (attention to goal tokens averaged across all response tokens, all layers, and all heads), and perplexity (PPL).

\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:four-way} presents the five-way comparison. The central finding is that, under the single seed tested, all four fine-tuned variants converge to nearly identical performance. GAR-ours spans only 0.75 percentage points (pp) across ThreadGate (0.50), +PCGrad (0.50), Uniform Gate (0.51), and LoRA-only (0.50), and PPL is 2.78 for all four on IID evaluation. Neither per-layer gating (ThreadGate), elimination of gradient direction conflict (+PCGrad), nor content-only gating (Uniform Gate) provides any measurable benefit over LoRA-only fine-tuning.

The Base model confirms that LoRA fine-tuning is the operative variable: GAR-ours jumps from 0.01 to ${\sim}$0.50, while PPL improves from 4.23 to 2.78. Since all four variants share the same GAR loss ($\tau = 0.5$), convergence near the target is expected and does not demonstrate gating benefit. GAR-Dongre remains uniformly low (0.01--0.02) across all conditions, consistent with \citet{dongre2026attention}.

The teacher-forced GAR-ours of 0.50--0.51 stands in sharp contrast to autoregressive generation, where GAR-ours drops to 0.015--0.025 across all fine-tuned variants, a 20--33$\times$ gap. This gap is universal: it persists across all training strategies---gating, gradient conflict elimination, and LoRA-only fine-tuning alike. We analyze this distribution shift in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Five-way comparison on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct. IID evaluation uses teacher-forced UltraChat text; held-out uses 50 non-overlapping conversations. All four fine-tuned variants converge to within 0.75\,pp on GAR-ours; PPL is indistinguishable. GAR-O = GAR-ours; GAR-D = GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}.}
\label{tab:four-way}
\begin{tabular}{l ccc ccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{IID Evaluation}} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{Held-out Evaluation}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-7}
\textbf{Configuration} & GAR-O & GAR-D & PPL & GAR-O & GAR-D & PPL \\
\midrule
Base              & 0.01 & 0.00 & 4.23 & 0.01 & 0.00 & 4.34 \\
ThreadGate        & 0.50 & 0.02 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.02 & 2.78 \\
\;+\,PCGrad       & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 \\
Uniform Gate      & 0.51 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.51 & 0.01 & 2.78 \\
LoRA-only         & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_attention_heatmap.pdf}
  \caption{Per-turn GAR-ours under teacher-forced evaluation ($N$\,=\,45, $\pm$1\,std). Fine-tuned variants maintain GAR near $\tau$\,=\,0.5 with 7--8\% decay (T1$\to$T4); Base decays 80\%. T5 std inflated by truncated conversations. Despite this preservation, autoregressive GAR collapses to 0.015--0.025 for all variants (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}), distribution shift is the failure mode.}
  \label{fig:attention-heatmap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Inference-Time Attention Steering}\label{subsec:steering}

The preceding sections established that all training-time interventions fail uniformly: exposure bias collapses autoregressive GAR regardless of gating or gradient management. We now test whether inference-time attention manipulation can recover GAR without retraining. We apply an additive bias $\beta$ to goal-token attention logits at every layer of the v7 ThreadGate checkpoint during generation.

Figure~\ref{fig:steering} summarizes both evaluation protocols. Under autoregressive generation ($N$\,=\,5), low bias values ($\beta \in [0, 2]$) yield negligible GAR change (0.028\,$\to$\,0.062) with stable PPL (${\sim}$1.4). A phase transition emerges between $\beta$\,=\,2 and $\beta$\,=\,5: GAR jumps from 0.062 to 0.680 while PPL rises only to 1.91. At $\beta$\,=\,5, generation GAR (0.680) surpasses the unsteered teacher-forced baseline (0.535). Further increase to $\beta$\,=\,10 yields GAR\,=\,0.959 but degrades PPL to 4.22. Teacher-forced evaluation ($N$\,=\,200) confirms steering effectiveness, though PPL cost escalates sharply ($\beta$\,=\,10: PPL\,=\,505).

These results close the causal loop identified in \S\ref{sec:analysis}: exposure bias shifts attention away from goal tokens, GAR collapses, and manually correcting attention via $\beta$ bias restores it. The failure resides at the attention routing layer, and inference-time intervention circumvents exposure bias. Two caveats apply: generation evaluation uses only $N$\,=\,5 conversations due to compute constraints, so the precise phase-transition boundary requires finer $\beta$ sweeps. Steering trades fluency for goal recall; practical deployment would need a Pareto-optimal $\beta$.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_attention_steering.pdf}
  \caption{Inference-time attention steering: additive bias $\beta$ applied to goal-token attention logits. (a)~Under autoregressive generation ($N$\,=\,5), a phase transition occurs at $\beta \in [2, 5]$: GAR jumps from 0.062 to 0.680 with modest PPL cost (1.41$\to$1.91). At $\beta$\,=\,5, generation GAR (0.680) exceeds the unsteered teacher-forced baseline (0.535, dotted line). (b)~Under teacher forcing ($N$\,=\,200), steering is effective but PPL cost escalates sharply ($\beta$\,=\,10: PPL\,=\,505).}
  \label{fig:steering}
\end{figure}

